1. Introduction and scope

1.1 Agreement

This Master Software Licence and Services Agreement ("Agreement") governs the downloading, installation, access to and use of software, applications, online services, associated documentation and related functionality supplied by the Software Provider ("Provider", "we", "us" or "our").

It applies to products made available free of charge, purchased through a one-off payment, licensed for a fixed period, supplied on a subscription basis, or provided for evaluation or trial purposes.

The person or organisation acquiring or using a Product is referred to as the "Customer", "you" or "your".

1.2 Products covered

This Agreement may apply to, among other things:

- Desktop and mobile software;
- Web applications and cloud-hosted services;
- Digital forensic and investigative tools;
- Data recovery and storage utilities;
- Text editors and development software;
- File transfer, upload and management software;
- Games and entertainment software;
- Application programming interfaces and associated utilities;
- Future software products and related services designated by the Provider.

1.3 Contractual documents

The Agreement consists of these general terms together with any applicable Product Schedule, accepted order, subscription details, service description or separately agreed written terms.

An applicable Product Schedule may specify additional functionality, usage restrictions, technical requirements, service commitments and risk information.

Where documents conflict, individually negotiated written terms take precedence, followed by the applicable Product Schedule, followed by this Agreement, unless expressly agreed otherwise.

No provision takes precedence over mandatory rights or obligations imposed by applicable law.

1.4 Consumer and business customers

A "Consumer" is an individual acting wholly or mainly outside their trade, business, craft or profession.

A "Business Customer" is any customer acting for purposes relating to their trade, business, craft or profession, including a company, partnership, public authority or individual acting in a business capacity.

Clauses expressly marked "Business Customers only" do not apply to Consumers.

1.5 Acceptance

You accept this Agreement by expressly agreeing to it during purchase, registration, installation or account creation, or by another legally effective method of acceptance.

Where a contract is formed before installation or first use, the terms made available and accepted at the time of contracting will apply.

You should retain a copy of the Agreement.

If you do not accept these terms, you should not acquire or use the relevant Product, subject to any rights you already have under applicable law.

2. Licence grant

2.1 Limited licence

Subject to this Agreement and the applicable Product Schedule, the Provider grants you a limited, non-exclusive licence to install, access and use the Product for its documented purposes.

The licence is non-transferable and non-sublicensable, except where expressly permitted by the applicable terms or required by law.

2.2 Licence conditions

The scope of your licence may be restricted by:

- Licence duration or subscription period;
- Number of authorised users or devices;
- Permitted commercial or non-commercial use;
- Licensed features or product editions;
- Technical or operational limits;
- Applicable geographic or organisational restrictions.

Restrictions must be communicated before purchase where material.

2.3 Ownership

Unless expressly stated otherwise, the Product is licensed rather than sold.

The Provider and its licensors retain ownership of their intellectual property in the Product. You retain ownership of your own data, documents and other content.

2.4 Open-source components

Components supplied under separate open-source licences remain subject to those licences.

Nothing in this Agreement restricts rights expressly granted by an applicable open-source licence or rights that cannot lawfully be restricted.

Where an entire Product is distributed under an open-source licence, that licence governs the rights granted under it.

3. Authorised use and restrictions

3.1 Lawful use

You must use the Product lawfully and within the scope of your licence.

You are responsible for ensuring that you have the necessary authority, permissions and legal basis to access, examine, modify, transfer, recover or process any systems, devices, files or information involved in your use of the Product.

3.2 Prohibited activities

Except where expressly permitted by law or the applicable licence, you must not:

a. Use the Product for unlawful access to systems, devices, accounts or data.

b. Use the Product to infringe intellectual property rights, privacy rights or other legal rights.

c. Deliberately interfere with the security, availability or operation of systems or services without appropriate authorisation.

d. Circumvent licence activation, access controls or technical restrictions unlawfully.

e. Redistribute proprietary components beyond the rights granted by the applicable licence.

f. Use hosted services to distribute malicious software, fraudulent material or unlawful content.

g. Exceed documented service limits in a way that materially disrupts the Provider's infrastructure or other customers.

h. Misrepresent, falsify or knowingly manipulate outputs in a manner intended to deceive others.

3.3 Modifications

Unauthorised modifications may affect functionality, compatibility, security and reliability.

The Provider is not responsible for failures caused by modifications made without its authorisation, except to the extent that the failure is attributable to the Provider or liability otherwise arises under applicable law.

This does not restrict lawful interoperability, security research, reverse engineering or modification rights where those rights cannot legally be excluded.

3.4 Customer responsibility

You are responsible for how you use the Product and for decisions made using its outputs.

Where the Product assists with technical, investigative or operational decisions, you should apply appropriate professional judgement and independent verification.

The Product does not transfer your legal responsibilities to the Provider.

4. Performance, capabilities and advertised claims

4.1 Internal testing

Unless expressly identified otherwise, published performance measurements, accuracy statistics, compatibility figures, benchmarks, recovery rates and technical capabilities may be based on testing performed internally by the Provider.

Such testing may use particular hardware, operating systems, configurations, datasets, workloads or environmental conditions.

4.2 Variability of results

Actual results may differ because of factors including:

- Hardware and software configuration;
- Data characteristics and condition;
- Operating system and driver behaviour;
- Storage media condition;
- Network capacity and reliability;
- Third-party dependencies;
- User configuration and operating procedures;
- Product version and external environmental conditions.

4.3 No universal performance guarantee

A benchmark, demonstration or example does not automatically constitute a guarantee that equivalent results will be achieved in all environments.

Any expressly agreed performance commitment, contractual specification or applicable statutory requirement remains binding.

4.4 Accuracy and interpretation

The Provider does not represent that every analysis, classification, detection, conversion, recovery or generated output will be complete or error-free.

Outputs may contain omissions, false positives, false negatives, inaccuracies or information requiring further verification.

This provision does not excuse a failure to meet an expressly agreed specification, a misleading product description or a duty imposed by law.

4.5 Accuracy of promotional information

The Provider will take reasonable care to ensure that factual product claims are supported by appropriate evidence.

Information about relevant testing conditions, limitations or assumptions will be made available where material to understanding advertised performance.

5. Customer data and backups

5.1 Customer ownership

You retain ownership of your files, documents, evidence, databases, uploaded content and other information processed using the Product ("Customer Data").

5.2 Permission to process

Where necessary to provide a hosted Product, you grant the Provider a limited permission to store, transmit, process, reproduce and otherwise handle Customer Data solely to provide the contracted service and for other lawful purposes transparently described in the applicable terms and Privacy Notice.

This permission does not transfer ownership of Customer Data.

5.3 Backups and precautions

You should maintain appropriate backups of important information.

Before performing destructive, irreversible, investigative or recovery operations, you should take precautions appropriate to the risks and follow documented procedures.

A Product should not be regarded as a replacement for a suitable backup or disaster recovery arrangement unless that functionality is expressly included in its specification.

5.4 Data corruption and loss

Software operations may involve risks of data loss, corruption, overwriting, incomplete processing or incompatibility.

The Provider does not guarantee that every file or dataset can be preserved, recovered or restored.

Responsibility for any loss caused by a breach of contract, negligence or other legally actionable conduct remains subject to the applicable liability provisions and mandatory law.

5.5 Hosted data

For Products involving hosted storage or processing, the applicable Product Schedule will describe relevant retention periods, export facilities, deletion procedures and any included backup or recovery functionality.

The Provider will apply appropriate technical and organisational security measures having regard to the nature of the service and applicable legal obligations.

6. Online services, availability and security

6.1 Service availability

Online services may occasionally be unavailable because of maintenance, technical faults, security incidents, infrastructure failures, connectivity problems or circumstances beyond the Provider's reasonable control.

Unless a specific availability commitment is agreed, the Provider does not promise uninterrupted or continuous access.

6.2 Reasonable service operation

The Provider will operate the service in accordance with its contractual commitments and applicable legal duties.

Where practicable, advance notice will be given of scheduled maintenance expected to materially affect availability.

6.3 Third-party services

A Product may depend on third-party infrastructure, operating systems, networks, libraries or services.

The Provider cannot control every aspect of third-party systems, but remains responsible for its own contractual and legal obligations.

6.4 Security limitations

No software or online system can be guaranteed to be entirely free from vulnerabilities or security incidents.

The Provider does not promise absolute security but will comply with its applicable security, contractual and data protection obligations.

6.5 Incident response

Security incidents will be assessed and handled in accordance with applicable law and the Provider's relevant contractual obligations.

Where required, affected parties and regulators will be notified.

7. Updates, changes and compatibility

7.1 Updates

The Provider may release updates, patches, enhancements and security fixes.

Some updates may be necessary for security, continued compatibility or lawful operation.

7.2 Compatibility

Unless expressly stated in the relevant Product Schedule, the Provider does not undertake to maintain compatibility indefinitely with every operating system, device, hardware platform or third-party dependency.

7.3 Material changes

The Provider will not use this clause to remove material paid-for functionality or substantially reduce an agreed service without appropriate contractual justification.

Where a material adverse change is proposed, affected customers will receive reasonable notice and, where required by law or the applicable contract, an appropriate right to cancel or obtain a remedy.

7.4 End of support

Product support and lifecycle commitments, where offered, will be described in the applicable Product Schedule.

The Provider may discontinue support in accordance with those commitments and applicable law.

8. Charges, free licences, trials and subscriptions

8.1 Free Products

Certain Products may be supplied without charge.

A free licence does not automatically include technical support, hosted storage, continued development or future upgrades.

Any applicable statutory rights remain unaffected.

8.2 Paid Products

Prices, licence duration, permitted use and included services will be communicated before purchase.

A one-off purchase does not automatically include unlimited upgrades, support or cloud services unless expressly stated.

8.3 Subscriptions

For subscriptions, the Provider will disclose the billing period, applicable charges, renewal arrangements, minimum commitment and cancellation procedure before the Customer enters into the contract.

Subscriptions renew only in accordance with the agreed terms and applicable law.

8.4 Trials

Trial licences may impose disclosed restrictions on duration, functionality, storage, usage or other resources.

Unless expressly agreed, expiry of a free trial does not itself authorise the Provider to impose undisclosed charges.

Any automatic conversion into a paid subscription must comply with applicable law and the information provided before registration.

8.5 Cancellation

Customers may cancel subscriptions using the cancellation arrangements disclosed at purchase and through the available account-management or customer-service facilities.

Consumer cancellation rights will comply with applicable law, including statutory cooling-off rights, renewal requirements and refund entitlements.

8.6 Failed payment

Where payment is overdue, the Provider may suspend relevant paid functionality after providing appropriate notice and an opportunity to resolve the issue, except where more immediate action is justified.

Any suspension remains subject to applicable legal obligations.

8.7 Refunds

Refunds will be provided where required by law or the applicable refund policy.

Nothing in this Agreement excludes statutory rights to a refund, price reduction, repair, replacement, repeat performance or another applicable remedy.

9. Consumer rights and warranties

This clause applies to Consumers.

9.1 Statutory protection

Nothing in this Agreement removes or restricts your rights under applicable consumer protection law.

Where the Consumer Rights Act 2015 applies, digital content must meet the relevant statutory requirements concerning satisfactory quality, fitness for purpose and correspondence with description.

Services must be provided with reasonable care and skill.

9.2 Technical limitations

Software may contain defects or experience compatibility and performance limitations.

However, this does not remove the Provider's responsibility to meet applicable statutory requirements.

9.3 Remedies

Where a Product does not meet applicable legal requirements, you may be entitled to remedies, including repair, replacement, price reduction, repeat performance, compensation or a refund, depending on the circumstances and the relevant legal provisions.

9.4 Damage to devices or other digital content

Where software causes damage to your device or other digital content in circumstances covered by section 46 of the Consumer Rights Act 2015, your statutory rights remain unaffected, including where the software was supplied free of charge.

9.5 Cancellation rights

Where statutory cancellation rights apply to a distance or off-premises contract, the Provider will comply with the relevant information, consent, cancellation and refund requirements.

Supplying digital content immediately does not automatically remove a Consumer's cancellation rights. Any legally permitted loss of those rights requires the applicable consent, acknowledgement and confirmation process.

9.6 Priority

If any other provision of this Agreement conflicts with your mandatory consumer rights, those rights prevail.

10. Business warranties and disclaimers

This clause applies to Business Customers only.

10.1 Contractual specification

The Provider will supply the Product substantially in accordance with any express contractual specification, subject to the agreed limitations and dependencies.

10.2 No additional performance warranty

Except for express contractual commitments and obligations that cannot lawfully be excluded or restricted, the Product is supplied on an "as is" and "as available" basis.

10.3 No guaranteed outcome

The Provider does not warrant that:

a. The Product will satisfy every requirement particular to the Business Customer.

b. Operation will be uninterrupted or entirely error-free.

c. All faults or vulnerabilities will be identified or corrected.

d. Every analysis, investigation, restoration or recovery will succeed.

e. Every supported third-party dependency will remain compatible indefinitely.

f. The Product will achieve a particular commercial, legal, technical or investigative outcome.

10.4 Implied terms

To the extent legally permissible and reasonable under applicable law, terms and warranties that would otherwise be implied are excluded, except insofar as they cannot lawfully be excluded.

10.5 Evaluation

The Business Customer is responsible for assessing the suitability of the Product for its intended operational environment.

Where relevant, the Business Customer should conduct appropriate acceptance testing, backup verification and risk assessment before relying on the Product for critical operations.

11. Limitation of liability: Business Customers

This clause applies to Business Customers only.

11.1 Non-excludable liability

Nothing in this Agreement excludes or restricts liability for:

a. Death or personal injury caused by negligence.

b. Fraud or fraudulent misrepresentation.

c. Any other liability that cannot lawfully be excluded or restricted.

11.2 Excluded commercial losses

Subject to clause 11.1 and the requirements of applicable law, the Provider will not be liable to a Business Customer for:

a. Loss of profits, revenue or anticipated savings.

b. Loss of business opportunities or contracts.

c. Loss of goodwill or reputation.

d. Business interruption or loss of production.

e. Indirect or consequential losses.

These exclusions are intended to apply whether the specified commercial losses arise directly or indirectly, insofar as such exclusions are legally enforceable and reasonable in the circumstances.

11.3 Direct loss

Liability for direct, legally recoverable loss not excluded under clause 11.2, including recoverable direct costs of restoring damaged data, remains subject to the aggregate liability cap in clause 11.4.

11.4 Aggregate liability cap

Subject to clause 11.1, the Provider's total aggregate liability to the Business Customer arising from or relating to the relevant Product, whether in contract, tort (including negligence), breach of statutory duty or otherwise, shall not exceed the greater of:

a. The total fees paid or payable by the Business Customer for that Product during the 12 months immediately preceding the event first giving rise to the claim; or

b. £[BUSINESS LIABILITY FLOOR].

This cap applies collectively to claims arising from the same or related events, rather than separately to each legal cause of action.

11.5 Reasonableness

The parties acknowledge that the allocation of commercial risk should be considered in light of the nature and price of the Product, any negotiated commitments, available remedies, insurance and the potential consequences of failure.

This clause applies only insofar as it satisfies the requirements of applicable law, including the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.

11.6 Mitigation

Each party must take reasonable steps to mitigate losses it suffers.

11.7 No exclusion of deliberate misconduct

Nothing in this clause is intended to exclude liability for deliberate wrongdoing by the Provider where such exclusion would be unlawful.

12. Limitation of liability: Consumers

This clause applies to Consumers.

12.1 No restriction of mandatory rights

The Provider does not exclude or restrict liability where doing so would be unlawful or unfair under applicable consumer protection law.

12.2 Foreseeable losses

The Provider is responsible for foreseeable loss or damage caused by its breach of this Agreement or failure to exercise reasonable care and skill where liability arises under applicable law.

Loss or damage is foreseeable where it was an obvious consequence of the breach or was reasonably contemplated by both parties when the contract was made.

12.3 Unrelated business losses

Products acquired by Consumers are supplied for private use unless otherwise agreed.

The Provider is not responsible under a Consumer contract for losses arising exclusively from a separate trade, business or professional activity that was outside the purpose of the contract, subject to applicable law.

12.4 No blanket exclusion

No provision in this Agreement should be interpreted as excluding all responsibility for faulty software, defective digital content, negligent services or legally compensable damage.

13. Misuse, responsibility and indemnification

13.1 Misuse

You are responsible for losses resulting from your unlawful conduct, deliberate misuse or breach of this Agreement to the extent recognised by applicable law.

The Provider is not responsible for losses caused solely by such conduct where the Provider has not otherwise incurred legal responsibility.

13.2 Business indemnity

Business Customers only.

The Business Customer shall indemnify the Provider against reasonable and properly substantiated losses, damages, liabilities and legal costs arising from third-party claims to the extent caused by:

a. The Business Customer's unlawful use of the Product;

b. The Business Customer's infringement of third-party rights through material it supplies or processes;

c. The Business Customer's intentional misuse of the Product; or

d. The Business Customer's material breach of the authorised-use restrictions.

The indemnity does not apply to the extent that a claim arises from the Provider's own negligence, breach of contract, unlawful conduct or other legally attributable fault.

13.3 Indemnity procedure

The Provider will, where reasonably practicable:

a. Notify the Business Customer promptly of a relevant claim.

b. Provide reasonable information concerning the claim.

c. Avoid incurring unnecessary or unreasonable costs.

d. Allow the Business Customer appropriate involvement in the defence or settlement of the claim.

No settlement imposing obligations on the Business Customer may be agreed without its prior consent, not to be unreasonably withheld.

13.4 Consumer position

Consumers are not subject to the business indemnity.

Consumers remain responsible for their own unlawful conduct under applicable law, but are not required by this Agreement to provide an open-ended indemnity for third-party claims.

14. Suspension and termination

14.1 Suspension

The Provider may suspend access where reasonably necessary to:

a. Address a material security threat.

b. Prevent serious misuse or unlawful activity.

c. Protect the integrity or availability of a hosted service.

d. Respond to a lawful requirement.

e. Enforce a material contractual obligation following appropriate notice where practicable.

14.2 Notice

Where reasonable and lawful, the Provider will explain the reason for suspension and provide an opportunity to remedy the issue.

Immediate suspension may occur where delay would create material security, legal or operational risks.

14.3 Termination for breach

Either party may terminate the Agreement for a material breach by the other party that cannot be remedied, or that remains unremedied after reasonable written notice and an opportunity to remedy it.

14.4 Expiry

Fixed-term licences and subscriptions may expire in accordance with their agreed terms.

14.5 Consequences

On termination or expiry, rights to use relevant proprietary Products may cease, subject to surviving licence rights, applicable law and any agreed post-termination access.

14.6 Customer Data

For hosted services, the applicable Product Schedule will describe the available data export and deletion arrangements.

Where reasonably practicable, Customers will have an opportunity to retrieve their data before deletion, subject to legitimate security restrictions, legal obligations and the agreed service specification.

14.7 Refunds

Termination does not remove refund rights or other remedies arising under applicable law.

15. Specialist software and higher-risk activities

15.1 Additional schedules

Products involving particular operational or legal risks may be subject to additional Product Schedules.

15.2 Digital forensic tools

Forensic software may assist with evidence acquisition, analysis, interpretation, reporting or preservation.

Outputs may be incomplete, affected by environmental conditions or require independent validation.

The Provider does not guarantee that a particular output will be accepted by a court, regulator or other decision-maker.

Nothing in this clause excuses failure to meet an expressly agreed specification or applicable legal duty.

15.3 Data recovery software

The ability to recover data depends on factors including physical media condition, overwriting, encryption, file-system damage and storage technology.

Recovery success is not guaranteed unless expressly agreed in respect of a defined operation or service.

15.4 File handling and transfer

File operations may be affected by permissions, interrupted transfers, storage failures, incompatible formats and other technical conditions.

Customers should use documented verification and backup procedures where appropriate.

15.5 Investigative and security-sensitive applications

Customers must ensure that investigative activity, data acquisition and system examination are properly authorised.

Possession of a licence does not itself grant authority to access third-party devices, systems or information.

16. Intellectual property and user-generated content

16.1 Provider intellectual property

The Provider retains ownership of its proprietary software, documentation, trademarks and other protected materials.

16.2 Customer work

The Provider does not acquire ownership of documents, source code, creative works, investigative reports or other original materials created by a Customer using the Product merely because the Product was used in their creation.

16.3 Uploaded materials

Customers must have appropriate rights or authorisation to upload and process content using hosted services.

16.4 Feedback

Customers may voluntarily submit suggestions or reports.

The Provider may use general suggestions to improve its Products, provided it does not thereby acquire ownership of Customer Data or confidential material or disregard applicable privacy or intellectual property obligations.

17. Privacy and data protection

17.1 Personal information

Personal data will be handled in accordance with applicable data protection law, including the UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018 where relevant.

17.2 Privacy Notice

The Provider's Privacy Notice will explain the processing of personal data for which it acts as controller.

17.3 Data processing agreements

Where the Provider processes personal data on behalf of a Business Customer as a processor, the parties will enter into an appropriate Data Processing Agreement containing the terms required by applicable law.

17.4 Statutory duties

Nothing in this Agreement excludes statutory data protection duties or liabilities where exclusion is not legally permitted.

17.5 Sensitive information

Where a Product handles evidential, confidential, special-category or otherwise sensitive information, the relevant Product Schedule may impose additional security, access, handling and retention requirements.

18. Events beyond reasonable control

Neither party shall be liable for delay or non-performance caused by events genuinely beyond its reasonable control, except in relation to obligations that remain enforceable notwithstanding such events.

A party relying on this clause must take reasonable steps to minimise disruption and resume performance.

This clause does not automatically excuse failures caused by inadequate precautions, lack of reasonable care or circumstances within a party's control.

Where a prolonged interruption materially defeats the purpose of an ongoing contract, the Customer may exercise any applicable contractual or statutory termination and refund rights.

19. Changes to the Agreement

19.1 Existing purchases

The terms applicable to a completed purchase will not be retrospectively changed merely by publishing a revised Agreement.

19.2 Continuing services

The Provider may propose changes to terms governing continuing services for legitimate reasons, including legal compliance, security or changes in the delivery of those services.

Material changes will be communicated with reasonable advance notice.

19.3 Customer options

Where changes materially disadvantage a Consumer, the Provider will provide appropriate cancellation and refund rights as required by law.

Continued use will not automatically be treated as acceptance of a material contractual change where valid consent is legally required.

20. General provisions

20.1 Severability

If a provision is found invalid or unenforceable, the remaining provisions will continue to apply so far as they can lawfully and practically operate independently.

20.2 Entire agreement

For Business Customers, this Agreement and the applicable contractual documents constitute the agreement between the parties concerning the relevant Product.

Nothing in this clause excludes liability for fraudulent misrepresentation or restricts another remedy where doing so would be unlawful.

This clause does not remove binding contractual statements or mandatory Consumer rights.

20.3 No waiver

Failure to enforce a provision immediately does not automatically waive the right to enforce it subsequently.

20.4 Assignment

Neither party may transfer its contractual obligations without any consent required by law or the Agreement.

The Provider may transfer the Agreement as part of a legitimate business restructuring or sale where doing so does not materially prejudice the Customer's contractual or statutory rights.

20.5 Third-party rights

Unless expressly provided otherwise, no person other than a party to this Agreement may enforce it under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.

This does not affect rights available independently of that Act.

20.6 Notices and contact

The Provider's contact details are set out at the beginning of this Agreement.

Contractual notices may be delivered using the methods specified in the applicable Product Schedule, subject to any statutory requirements.

21. Governing law and disputes

21.1 Governing law

This Agreement is governed by the laws of England and Wales, subject to mandatory legal protections that apply to a Consumer under applicable law.

21.2 Business disputes

For Business Customers, the courts of England and Wales shall have exclusive jurisdiction, except where a mandatory legal provision requires otherwise.

21.3 Consumer disputes

Consumers may exercise their rights to bring proceedings in courts available to them under applicable law.

Nothing in this Agreement removes mandatory protections available to Consumers in their country of habitual residence where those protections apply.

21.4 Informal resolution

Before commencing proceedings, the parties are encouraged, but are not required, to attempt a reasonable informal resolution.

This does not prevent urgent legal action or the exercise of statutory rights.

SCHEDULE A — DIGITAL FORENSICS AND INVESTIGATIVE SOFTWARE

This Schedule applies only to Products identified as forensic, investigative or evidential tools.

A1. Intended purpose

The Product is intended to assist suitably authorised users with specified investigative or technical tasks.

It is not a substitute for professional training, appropriate forensic methodology, legal advice or independent evidential assessment.

A2. Forensic accuracy

Results may be affected by file-system behaviour, operating system artefacts, storage characteristics, incomplete evidence, damaged data, encryption, parsing limitations and software defects.

The Provider does not warrant that every relevant artefact will be identified or that every result will be free from error.

A3. Evidence verification

The Customer is responsible for applying suitable procedures to verify the integrity, provenance and interpretation of evidence.

Such procedures may include hashing, independent analysis, validation of timestamps, preservation of original data and appropriate audit records.

A4. Evidential admissibility

The Provider does not guarantee that a report, extracted artefact or other output will be admissible or accepted as conclusive evidence in legal or disciplinary proceedings.

Admissibility and evidential weight depend on the relevant legal framework and circumstances.

A5. Chain of custody

Unless expressly included in the Product's documented functionality, the Product does not independently guarantee compliance with chain-of-custody requirements.

The Customer is responsible for maintaining appropriate records of evidence handling.

A6. Independent decisions

Investigative decisions, formal allegations and conclusions should not be based solely on unverified software output where independent validation is reasonably necessary.

A7. Lawful authority

The Customer must possess the necessary authority to examine or process relevant systems and information.

SCHEDULE B — DATA RECOVERY, STORAGE AND FILE OPERATIONS

This Schedule applies to Products designed to recover, manipulate, convert, transfer, synchronise or manage data.

B1. Recovery limitations

Data recovery may be unsuccessful even where the Product operates as designed.

No particular quantity, percentage or category of data is guaranteed to be recoverable unless expressly agreed.

B2. Existing damage

The Provider is not responsible for pre-existing storage damage, corruption or inaccessible information.

B3. Operational precautions

The Customer should follow applicable instructions concerning backup, source preservation, storage integrity and recovery destinations.

Where appropriate, recovery operations should avoid writing to the original affected storage medium.

B4. Storage and transfer

Operations may be interrupted by hardware failure, network loss, permissions, resource exhaustion or third-party service limitations.

B5. Verification

The Customer should independently verify the completeness and integrity of important recovered or transferred data.

B6. Liability

This Schedule does not exclude liability for damage attributable to the Provider's own breach of contract, negligence or failure to meet applicable legal obligations.

SCHEDULE C — HOSTED SERVICES AND FILE UPLOADS

This Schedule applies to cloud-hosted software and hosted data-processing services.

C1. Service description

The applicable Product Schedule will identify:

- Hosting and storage functionality;
- Applicable storage and transfer limits;
- Retention and deletion periods;
- Backup and recovery commitments;
- Security arrangements;
- Data export facilities;
- Technical support and service commitments.

C2. Customer content

The Customer remains responsible for having the rights and permissions necessary to upload and process content.

C3. Service integrity

The Provider will take reasonable measures to maintain service integrity and comply with agreed security commitments.

C4. Content restrictions

The Provider may restrict or remove content where reasonably necessary to comply with law, enforce lawful usage restrictions or address serious security threats.

C5. Data retention and deletion

Customer Data will be retained and deleted in accordance with the applicable Product Schedule, Privacy Notice, Data Processing Agreement and legal obligations.

C6. Service termination

Where practicable and legally permissible, customers will be given access to available export facilities before data is permanently deleted following termination.

